{"question": "A 77-year-old woman presents to the emergency room with the complaints of fever, malaise, and night sweats. She recently observed an enlargement of her axillary lymph nodes, which she examines on a weekly basis. She has a remote history of breast cancer in her 60s that was treated with radiation and chemotherapy. She also reports a history of extensive travel to Africa and a 30-pack-year history of smoking. On physical exam, several axillary lymph nodes are palpable with a large non-tender palpable mass in her right axilla measuring 10 x 8 cm. Fine-needle aspiration demonstrates what the pathologist describes as \"a centroblastic and immunoblastic cell presence, suspicious for non-Hodgkin\u2019s lymphoma (NHL)\u2013diffuse large B cell variant\". Which of the following risk factors is responsible for this patient\u2019s condition?", "options": {"A": "Previous radiation therapy", "B": "Previous breast cancer", "C": "Female gender", "D": "Travel to Africa", "E": "Axillary lymph node involvement"}, "pred_answer": "A", "gold_answer": "A", "question_domains": ["Hematology", "Oncology", "Infectious Disease", "Radiation Oncology", "General Surgery"], "option_domains": ["Oncology", "Infectious Disease"], "question_analyses": {"Hematology": "The patient's condition is highly suggestive of a recurrence or progression of lymphoproliferative disease, likely in the form of a non-Hodgkin\u2019s lymphoma (NHL) \u2013 diffuse large B cell variant. The key aspects of her presentation that are most alarming and noteworthy include:\n\n1. **Fever, Malaise, and Night Sweats**: These symptoms are indicative of a systemic inflammatory or neoplastic process, which can be associated with lymphoma. They are often referred to as \"B symptoms\" in lymphoma, which are prognostically significant. 2. **Lymph Node Enlargement**: The palpable and enlarged axillary lymph nodes, particularly the large mass in the right axilla, are a critical finding. Lymphadenopathy is a hallmark of lym