 normal range but suggest mild liver involvement. The alkaline phosphatase (68 U/L) is also within the normal range, which might suggest that the obstruction is not severe enough to cause significant cholestasis. However, the slight elevation in bilirubin and liver enzymes could be indicative of bile stasis or mild hepatocellular injury. The abdominal ultrasonography was inconclusive, which is not uncommon in cases of acute cholecystitis or biliary obstruction where the gallbladder may be distended and difficult to visualize clearly..", "Emergency Medicine": "The scenario presented involves a 42-year-old woman with a 2-day history of right upper abdominal pain and nausea, along with a fever of 38.5\u00b0C (101.3\u00b0F). Her physical examination reveals a distended abdomen and right upper quadrant tenderness, which is consistent with inflammation or obstruction in the biliary system. The laboratory findings, including a leukocyte count of 14,000/mm\u00b3, suggest an inflammatory process. The liver function tests (LFTs) show slightly elevated AST (32 U/L) and ALT (40 U/L), but the total bilirubin is within normal limits, and the alkaline phosphatase is only mildly elevated at 68 U/L. These findings are not highly indicative of severe liver disease but do suggest some degree of hepatobiliary involvement. The abdominal ultrasonography was inconclusive, which is not uncommon in cases of acute cholangitis or biliary obstruction where the gallbladder may not be distended or where there is significant bowel gas obscuring the view. Cholescintigraphy, which is a nuclear medicine test, showed visualization of the intrahepatic bile ducts, hepatic ducts, common bile duct, and proximal small bowel, indicating that there is no obstruction at the level of the common bile duct or proximal small bowel..", "Hepatology": "The scenario presented involves a 42-year-old woman with a 2-day history of right upper abdominal pain and nausea, along with a high body mass index (BMI) of 34 kg/m